orces  backed by the United Arab Emirates over  the weekend took over the southern  capital Aden and they ultimately would  like to have their own state so they are  coming to blows with the forces of the  formally recognized president Hedy and  it's very unclear how this might end  right now sings a calm in the port city  of Aden in the south but they could  easily escalate again because the  southern forces are refusing to withdraw  from the military positions that they've  taken and although they've agreed to a  ceasefire and talks the president heard  he is saying well I won't have talks  unless you withdraw from your military  positions first so it's still very  volatile but that said Leila and I  should say that the leadership at the  level of the UAE leader Mohammed bin  Zayed and the Saudi leader King Salman  and the Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman  they're very unlikely to fall out  publicly they're going to paper over  these cracks and put a good cosmetic  face on it this this is such a nice  segue because I really want us to focus  on on that and take our conversation to  that Saudi UAE alliance because Abu  Dhabi's crown prince has was in Saudi  this week to calm tensions and the  optics were you know there's no trouble  here  we want all the warring parties to come  to the negotiating table but what is the  likelihood of the warring tables that  putting down their arms and engaging in  a political process and were you  convinced by the UAE leader trying to  smooth over the differences that have  arisen with Saudi well that's a very  potent question in fact the statement  that came out of their meeting in Mecca  on Monday was very general it talked  about brotherly love and mutual interest  and shared goals but it didn't go into  any of the detail about the future shape  of Yemen  and what both of their end goals are  inside that country and although it's  very unlikely that the leaders  themselves will fall out indeed they  haven't fallen out they don'